# Dart Coding Style

## Types and Readability

- Use explicit types for public APIs and non-obvious locals.
- Avoid `dynamic` in application code; prefer concrete typed structures.
- Prefer `final` for values assigned once.
- Use `var` only when the right-hand side makes the type obvious and readability
  improves.
- Prefer small functions and early returns over deep nesting.

## Naming

- Use `UpperCamelCase` for types, enums, typedefs, and extensions.
- Use `lowerCamelCase` for variables, methods, parameters, constants, and enum
  values.
- Use `lowercase_with_underscores` for files and directories.
- Use verb-based names for actions and `is/has/can/should/needs` prefixes for
  booleans.
- Use leading underscores only for private library members.
- Avoid vague names such as `helper`, `manager`, `data`, and `process` when a
  more specific name exists.

## Imports and File Shape

- Order imports as `dart:`, then `package:`, then relative imports.
- Sort imports alphabetically within each group.
- Place `export` directives after imports.
- Avoid the legacy `library` directive unless the file intentionally needs it.
- Use `show` only when it materially improves clarity.

## Functions and Parameters

- Keep functions small and single-purpose.
- Use arrow syntax only for simple single-expression members.
- Use named parameters for optional or readability-sensitive inputs.
- Prefer required named parameters for mandatory values with unclear position.
- Prefer local named functions over assigning a lambda to a variable when the
  closure deserves a name.

## Strings and Collections

- Prefer string interpolation over concatenation.
- Prefer typed collection literals such as `List<T>`, `Map<K, V>`, and `Set<T>`.
- Avoid raw `List`, `Map`, and `Set` in application code.
- Prefer `isEmpty` and `isNotEmpty` over comparing `.length`.
- Prefer declarative collection APIs when they improve clarity.
- Use `whereType<T>()` when filtering mixed collections by type.
- Use collection `if`, `for`, spread, and null-aware spread when they simplify
  the flow instead of hiding it.
- Use `StringBuffer` when building larger strings incrementally.

## Modern Dart

- Use pattern matching, records, and exhaustive `switch` constructs when they
  simplify the code.
- Use sealed classes when the domain has a fixed set of variants carrying
  meaningful data.
- Prefer tear-offs when the target signature matches exactly.
- Keep lambdas when adapting arguments makes intent clearer.
- Use modern features only when they improve clarity for the current SDK
  constraint and team readability.

## Async and Logging

- Capture the most specific failure you can actually handle.
- Preserve `error` and `stackTrace` when translating failures.
- Prefer explicit error boundaries over spreading `try/catch` everywhere.
- Do not use `print` in library/runtime code; use the project's logging
  abstraction or structured logging instead.

## Formatting and Tooling

- Format touched code with `dart format`.
- Prefer `dart fix --apply` for safe automated cleanups when appropriate.
- Run `dart analyze` after meaningful changes, or the project-equivalent
  analyzer command when needed.
- Respect the project's configured lint baseline instead of introducing a second
  competing style policy.
- Trust the formatter for line wrapping and trailing commas.

